# Black-hole ringdown and the zeros of zeta

Ringdown frequencies on exact `sech²` black holes read the spectrum of the horizon. A round horizon
gives a ladder that is an `ℓ²` perturbation of an arithmetic progression, and no such ladder consists of
zeta zeros. A modular horizon carries every zero as a pole of a causal scattering symbol, the retarded
transform of an echo train with delays `2 log c`.

RH is equivalent to analyticity above the sharp quarter-line damping boundary. Functional-equation
partners have a fixed sum of damping rates. The raw echo train has unbounded arrival spikes;
Gaussian observation integrals converge absolutely, retain the exact source transform, and preserve every
arithmetic pole with its multiplicity.

On the massless topological AdS black hole with horizon `PSL₂(ℤ)\ℍ`, an Eisenstein wave packet lifts to
exact solutions of the conformally coupled wave equation of the metric, smooth across the future horizon.
Their expansion in damped modes has one mode per zero, on the minus branch only, with a remainder that
decays faster than every exponential, uniformly at late times. The reflected signal is the pulse convolved
with the echo train minus its mean growth, and the causal symbol is the transfer function of the field.

Every compact pulse gives an exact solution by the method of images, with the same echo identity and ringdown. Every
square-integrable function on the horizon is a constant plus a limit of such fields plus a cusp form, and the wave evolution
is a contractive cosine family on the complement of the constants. Cusp forms have `λ ≥ ¼` and undamped horizon modes, at the
universal rate of the boundary condition; the mirror-odd sector is cuspidal, so they exist; their eigenvalues form a discrete unbounded sequence. The whole mirror-odd half of the horizon is cuspidal: the zeros couple only to mirror-even data. Their number below `Λ` is at least a multiple of `Λ` (a Weyl lower bound) and at most a multiple of `Λ + 1` (a Weyl upper bound, by a pointwise Bessel inequality and the monotone decay of the cusp modes), so the heat trace of the cusp channel is finite. The scalar wave of a pulse carries an exact vacuum perturbation of the metric, in the
vector and in the scalar sector, in closed form and covariant under `SL₂(ℤ)`; each zero is a gravitational quasinormal mode, and
each component of the metric rings down in these modes.

The late-time decay exponent of the field of every pulse is half the width of the zero-free strip, at every strength, and the
thermalization exponent of long horocycles is `½` plus the same number: the two RH rates `e^{-τ/4}` and `y^{3/4}` are one exponent.
Extending the modular black hole across its bifurcate horizon `UV = 0` to the two-boundary Kruskal–Maldacena `AdS₄` spacetime (`β_H = 2π`), local prime-cycle gates `1 + Σ (1 - 1/p) e^{-w · 2(k+1) log p}` converge independently of `ζ` to `ζ(2w)/ζ(2w+1)` and identify every nontrivial zero of `ζ` with an isolated pole of exact multiplicity in both the retarded correlator `G_R` and the trans-horizon Wightman correlator `G_{RL}` on `0 < |Im ν| < ½` (`1/16` strip analyticity holds unconditionally; `1/4` is equivalent to RH). While reflection-paired Hadamard factorization forces global Lax–Phillips Schur passivity `‖ξ(2w)/ξ(1+2w)‖ ≤ 1` on `Re w ≥ 0`, replacing the prime-`2` gate by the retarded Schur factor `B_{2,α}(w)` (`½ < α < 1`) preserves causal echo support, rational-prime Euler structure, functional equation, Schur/Herglotz passivity, unitarity, and the complete `G_R`/`G_{RL}` zero-to-pole dictionary while collapsing the reflected-pulse decay rate to `(1 - α)/2 → 0` (and smooth 4D vacuum Einstein–`AdS₄` quaternion-cover black holes with locally tempered geodesic factors likewise violate `¼` via `0 < λ_j < ¼`).

Every cusp form is smooth and is a gravitational quasinormal mode, in both vector branches and the scalar sector, at exactly
the base damping of its sector; the zeta modes lie strictly deeper, exactly `¼` deeper iff RH, and form a set of density zero.

Each cusp space is finite-dimensional and carries the commuting Hecke operators `T_p`; every eigenvalue of the cusp spectrum
has a Hecke–Maass form, with the trivial bound `|λ_p| ≤ √p + 1/√p` on its normalized Hecke eigenvalues. A Hecke eigenform is determined by its
eigenvalues and its first Fourier modes `a_{±1}`, and each mode lies in a line, so every joint eigenspace of the Laplacian and
the Hecke operators has dimension at most two. The mirror commutes with all of them, and each joint eigenspace of definite
parity is a line, nonzero at every eigenvalue of the cusp spectrum (strong multiplicity one). Every cusp form carries an L-function `Σ ρ(n)|n|^{-s}`: entire, with completed function `¼π^{-s}Γ((s+ν)/2)Γ((s-ν)/2)L(s)` symmetric under `s ↦ 1 - s` (the Mellin transform of `y^{-1/2}g(iy)`, symmetric under `z ↦ -1/z`). For a Hecke eigenform the coefficients are multiplicative and `L(s) = (ρ(1)+ρ(-1)) Π_p (1 - λ_p p^{-s} + p^{-2s})⁻¹`. The coefficients are of size one on average, `Σ_{|n|≤N} |ρ(n)|² ≤ A N log N` (Rankin–Selberg), so `L` converges absolutely and has its Euler product on all of `Re s > 1`. The Eisenstein function, known to be automorphic on the critical line, is automorphic at every regular spectral parameter and its completion `ξ(1+2u)E/((1+2u)2u)` is even in `u` (identity theorem from the line). The energy density of a cusp mode pairs with the Eisenstein channel through its Rankin–Selberg series: `∫|g|²E(s-½) = π^{-s}/8 · Γ(s/2)²Γ(s/2+ν)Γ(s/2-ν)/Γ(s) · Σ|ρ(n)|²|n|^{-s}` for `Re s > 1`. The completed Rankin–Selberg function `∫|g|²E*(s-½)` is holomorphic on `ℂ∖{0,½,1}` and symmetric under `s ↦ 1-s`, so the Rankin–Selberg series continues to `Re s > ½` except `s = 1`; its further poles can only sit at zeros of `ξ(2s)`. Removing the pole at `s = 1` and applying Wiener–Ikehara gives the mean-square law `(1/N)Σ_{|n|<N}|ρ(n)|² → (24/π²) cosh(πt) ‖g‖²`: every horocyclic harmonic of a ringdown mode carries the same average energy. Abel summation then shows that long closed horocycles equidistribute that energy: `∫_{-1/2}^{1/2}|g(x+iy)|²dx → (3/π)‖g‖²` as `y → 0`. Its rate reads the zeros: a rate `y^α` forces `Λ_g(ρ/2) = 0` at every zero `ρ` of `ζ` with `Re ρ > 2 - 2α`, so a zero visible to `Λ_g` caps the rate at `y^{1 - Re ρ/2}`. The function `s(s-½)(s-1)Λ_g(s)` is entire, odd under `s ↦ 1-s`, and decays like `e^{-π|Im s|}` in vertical strips (Phragmén–Lindelöf). Mellin inversion of the horocycle energy and the contour shift then give the rate: `|m(y) - (3/π)‖g‖²| ≤ C y^{½-ε}` unconditionally and `C y^{¾-ε}` under the Riemann hypothesis. The normalized Rankin–Selberg function grows polynomially on `½ < Re s ≤ 5/4`, and log-Gaussian smoothed sums `Σ f(m)φ_Δ(m/X)` equal `A e^{Δ²} X e^{-1/X} + O(Δ^{-N-1}X^{3/4})`. Taking `Δ = X^{-β}` localizes single harmonics: Rankin's bound `|ρ(n)| ≤ C|n|^{½-δ}`.
